firedamp-api — Cloudflare Worker
================================

Proxies plume-analysis requests to OpenRouter, holding the API key as a Worker
secret so it never ships to the browser. Caches every response in D1 for free
re-serves and to build up a queryable dataset of plume → AI source attributions.

Endpoints
---------

POST /api/analyse
  Body: { plumeId, prompt, image (data URL), lat, lon, dt, rate, src }
  Streams Server-Sent Events compatible with the OpenRouter chat-completion
  shape. Response header X-Firedamp-Cache is "hit" when the row was served
  from D1 and "miss" when freshly generated.

GET /api/analyses?limit=200&since=<ms>
  Returns recent analyses as JSON. Origin-checked.

GET /healthz
  Liveness check.

First-time setup
----------------

  cd worker

  # 1. Create the D1 database (run once). Copy the printed database_id into
  #    wrangler.toml under [[d1_databases]].
  npx wrangler d1 create firedamp-analyses

  # 2. Apply the schema.
  npx wrangler d1 execute firedamp-analyses --file schema.sql --remote

  # 3. Set the OpenRouter key as a secret (will prompt for value).
  npx wrangler secret put OPENROUTER_KEY

  # 4. Deploy.
  npx wrangler deploy

The deployed Worker URL ends up at firedamp-api.<account>.workers.dev. Paste it
into web/index.html as <meta name="firedamp-api" content="…">.

Local development
-----------------

  npx wrangler dev

This serves the Worker on http://localhost:8787, talking to the *remote* D1.
For an isolated local DB add --local and use `wrangler d1 execute … --local`
to apply the schema there too.

Operational notes
-----------------

- Origin check (ALLOWED_ORIGINS) gates browser callers but Referer/Origin can
  be spoofed by a determined caller. The hard guard is the OpenRouter key's
  own credit limit — set one in the OpenRouter dashboard.
- Cache key is (plume_id, model, sha256(prompt)). When the prompt template
  changes we don't invalidate; old rows are kept and a new prompt produces a
  new row. The newest matching row wins on read.
- D1 free tier: 5 GB, 5M reads/day, 100k writes/day — far above what firedamp
  uses.
